---
import BlogPost from '../../layouts/BlogPost.astro';

const builds = [
  { name: 'S’mores (any time of year)', base: 'Vanilla Blizzard', ask: 'Graham cracker pieces, marshmallow topping and chocolate pieces' },
  { name: 'Butterbeer', base: 'Vanilla Blizzard', ask: 'Butterscotch topping and caramel, topped with whipped cream' },
  { name: 'Mocha', base: 'Chocolate Blizzard', ask: 'A shot of coffee from the MooLatté station and hot fudge' },
  { name: 'Peanut butter and jelly', base: 'Vanilla Blizzard', ask: 'Peanut butter topping and strawberry topping' },
  { name: 'Cookies and dough', base: 'Oreo Blizzard', ask: 'Add cookie dough pieces' },
  { name: 'Banana split in a cup', base: 'Vanilla Blizzard', ask: 'Banana, pineapple, strawberry and chocolate toppings' },
  { name: 'Misty Freeze', base: 'Misty Slush', ask: 'Blend in vanilla soft serve' },
  { name: 'Misty Float', base: 'Misty Slush', ask: 'Vanilla soft serve swirled on top instead of blended' },
];

const faqs = [
  { q: 'Does Dairy Queen have a secret menu?', a: 'Not officially. The "secret menu" is a set of custom orders made from ingredients DQ already stocks, such as extra Blizzard mix-ins or toppings. Order by listing the ingredients, not a nickname, because staff will not know names like "Butterbeer Blizzard."' },
  { q: 'How do I order a secret menu item at DQ?', a: 'Start with a regular menu item, such as a Small Vanilla Blizzard, then list the add-ins you want. Expect a small charge for each extra topping or mix-in.' },
  { q: 'Can you get two flavors in one Blizzard?', a: 'Yes. Most stores will blend two mix-ins into one Blizzard, for example Oreo and cookie dough. You will usually pay for the extra mix-in.' },
  { q: 'What is a Misty Freeze?', a: 'A Misty Freeze is a Misty Slush blended with vanilla soft serve. It used to be on the menu at many stores, and some locations will still make it if you ask.' },
];
---
<BlogPost
  title="Dairy Queen Secret Menu: Items to Order (2026)"
  description="Dairy Queen secret menu items you can order by asking for simple swaps and mix-ins, with exactly what to say at the counter."
  h1="Dairy Queen Secret Menu: What to Order and How to Ask"
  crumb="DQ Secret Menu"
  eyebrow="Custom orders and hacks"
  image="/Images/mixing-bowl-mashup-blizzard.webp"
  imageAlt="Dairy Queen Blizzard with mixed toppings"
  datePublished="2026-04-02"
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <p class="lead"><strong>Dairy Queen does not have an official secret menu.</strong> What people call the DQ secret menu is a list of custom orders built from ingredients the store already has: extra Blizzard mix-ins, sundae toppings and old favorites like the Misty Freeze. The trick is to order by ingredients, not by nickname. Here are the ones worth trying and exactly how to ask.</p>

  <h2 id="how-to-order">How to order off-menu at DQ</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Start with a real menu item</strong>, such as "a Small Vanilla Blizzard."</li>
    <li><strong>List the add-ins</strong> you want, one at a time.</li>
    <li><strong>Expect a small extra charge</strong> for each topping or mix-in.</li>
    <li><strong>Go when it is quiet.</strong> Custom orders are easier mid-afternoon than at the lunch or dinner rush.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>What a store can make depends on what it stocks. Treat-only DQ locations have fewer ingredients than DQ Grill &amp; Chill restaurants.</p>

  <h2 id="secret-blizzards">Secret menu Blizzards and treats</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="size-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Start with</th><th>Ask to add</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {builds.map((b) => (
          <tr><td><strong>{b.name}</strong></td><td>{b.base}</td><td>{b.ask}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>S’mores Blizzard, any time of year</h3>
  <p>The <a href="/items/smores-blizzard">S’mores Blizzard</a> is seasonal, but you can get close all year. Say: <em>"Can I get a Small Vanilla Blizzard with graham cracker pieces, marshmallow topping and chocolate pieces?"</em> Graham pieces are used in some cheesecake Blizzards, so many stores have them.</p>

  <h3>Butterbeer Blizzard</h3>
  <p>Ask for <em>"a Small Vanilla Blizzard with butterscotch and caramel, topped with whipped cream."</em> It is sweet, buttery and tastes a lot like the theme-park drink.</p>

  <h3>Mocha Blizzard</h3>
  <p>Coffee fans can ask for a chocolate Blizzard with a shot of coffee from the <a href="/items/moolatte">MooLatté</a> station and hot fudge. It is less sweet than most Blizzards. Not every store will add coffee, so ask nicely.</p>

  <h3>Two-flavor Blizzards</h3>
  <p>The easiest hack is combining two regular mix-ins. Oreo with cookie dough and <a href="/items/reeses-blizzard">Reese’s</a> with brownie are popular pairs. Start with your favorite from our <a href="/blog/best-blizzard-flavors-ranked">Blizzard rankings</a>.</p>

  <h3>Misty Freeze and Misty Float</h3>
  <p>These older drinks were removed from most menus, but the ingredients are still there. A <a href="/items/misty-slush">Misty Slush</a> blended with vanilla soft serve is a Misty Freeze. Ask for the soft serve on top instead of blended and you get a float.</p>

  <h2 id="savory">Savory hacks at DQ Grill &amp; Chill</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Loaded curds:</strong> order <a href="/items/cheese-curds">cheese curds</a> with a side of gravy for dipping, like a quick poutine.</li>
    <li><strong>Spicy strips:</strong> ask for FlameThrower sauce on the side with your <a href="/categories/chicken-strip-baskets">chicken strip basket</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Swap your side:</strong> you can ask to swap fries for onion rings or curds; some stores charge a little extra.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="etiquette">Be nice to the crew</h2>
  <p>Secret menu orders depend on the person making them. Keep it to two or three changes, avoid the rush hours, and do not argue about the extra charge. If a store says no, it is usually because they do not stock the ingredient.</p>
</BlogPost>
